1:# CLAUDE.md — Heavy Metal Index Wiki
14:## Part 1 — Strategic frame (read before making any decision)
16:### Why this wiki exists
22:### Three audience layers, in order of pricing power
32:### Deliberate architectural separation
36:### Database-rights positioning
40:## Part 2 — The wiki/HMT&C relationship (the single most important rule)
48:### Two directions of drift, both destroy defensibility equally
54:### The rule
60:## Part 3 — Products this wiki serves
78:## Part 4 — Architecture
98:**Version control.** The wiki is in git. Commit after every ingest, query, lint, or schema change. Commit messages follow the `log.md` format (see [[docs/conventions]]). Git history is the versioning mechanism; the `updated:` frontmatter field is a convenience, not the source of truth. When a wiki page is cited in future HMT&C documentation, litigation response, or regulatory submission, it must be possible to reconstruct exactly what the page said on a specific date. Do not skip commits because "nothing much changed." For bulk ingest runs, commit after each batch of 200 papers rather than per-paper; batch commits with descriptive batch reports preserve reconstructibility without overwhelming the log.
102:## Part 5 — Source-file handling
104:See [[docs/ingest-workflows]] for source-file handling: filesystem-handle vs cite-key, source-page creation, deduplication, pre-populated frontmatter, and existing-pages-aware ingest discipline (including the Phase 1 rule that empty `products: []` on a food/personal-care paper is a defect).
106:## Part 5b — Routing layer (system rule, not a per-session task)
110:### What routing is
114:The routing layer is the answer to a recurring failure mode: papers get ingested with frontmatter that correctly names the products and ingredients they touch, but no downstream process reads that frontmatter and fans the source out to the sibling pages. Without the routing layer, every later session has to re-derive routing from scratch, which is slow and lossy.
116:### Where routing lives
118:`data/evidence/product_source_routing_audit.csv` is the canonical routing artifact. One row per (source, target-page, route_kind) triple. The `route_kind` vocabulary is fixed: `direct_evidence`, `broad_formula_context`, `broad_powder_context`, `broad_product_context`, `exposure_only`, `regulatory_context`. Two sibling logs catch failures: `data/evidence/routing_unresolved.csv` (source frontmatter does not connect to any known target page) and `data/evidence/routing_malformed.csv` (source frontmatter is missing required fields).
120:### How routing is maintained
128:### What models are responsible for during ingest
130:The model writes the source page. It fills in frontmatter accurately, especially the `products`, `ingredients`, `matrices`, `metals`, and `jurisdictions` arrays, because those arrays are the routing layer's input. If a source touches infant formula generally without specifying soy vs non-soy, the frontmatter should say `matrices: [infant-formula]`, not `matrices: [infant-formula-powder-non-soy]`. The routing layer fans broad scopes out to sibling pages; the model does not pre-decide that fan-out by overspecifying frontmatter.
132:### What models are NOT responsible for during ingest
138:### What lint catches
144:## Part 6 — Page templates
148:## Part 7 — Audience layering
150:See [[docs/page-templates]] (Audience layering section). Every substantive page carries `audience: [regulator, educator, consumer, app]` in frontmatter; section-level `<!-- audience: ... -->` tagging allows one wiki to render three ways. Consumer translation must specify dose, population, and consumption frequency; never use undefined qualifiers ("small," "trace," "safe," "dangerous") without a quantitative anchor.
152:## Part 8 — Ingest workflows
156:## Part 9 — Synthesis and re-synthesis
162:### Trigger conditions
174:### Synthesis modes
182:### Downstream propagation rule
186:## Part 10 — Page creation and naming
192:## Part 11 — Priority ordering for bulk ingest
196:## Part 12 — Brand-level data: hard firewall
212:## Part 13 — Evidence grading
216:## Part 14 — Conventions
218:See [[docs/conventions]] for units (always ppb µg/kg unless source uses different units; wet vs dry weight always specified), metal names (Pb, Cd, iAs, tAs, MeHg, tHg, Ni, Al, Cr, Cr-VI, Sn, Sb, U with iAs/tAs and tHg/MeHg as non-negotiable distinctions), HMT&C analyte vocabulary (the official 10-analyte certification list: Pb, tAs, Cd, MeHg, tHg, iAs, Ni, Al, Cr-VI, Sn), dates (ISO-8601), cite-keys, wikilink frontmatter rules, numbers/regulatory-values/uncertainty discipline, and consumer language.
222:## Part 15 — Writing style for wiki content
226:## Parts 16-17 — `index.md` and `log.md` formats
230:## Part 18 — Lint
234:Lint runs across the wiki and checks: contradictions, stale claims, orphans, missing/malformed frontmatter, regulatory-value drift, silent threshold deviation (the Part 2 wiki/HMT&C drift check), evidence-tier mismatch, provenance gaps, the full routing-audit family (unresolved, malformed, stale, orphaned reference, missing-products, zero-routed-source, Step 0 Lock pending), the ingredient coverage/body-table/mandatory-section audits, the product-page mandatory-section audit, and the language-judgment checks (em dashes, inline bold, prose-vs-bullets, undefined consumer qualifiers) that run as the delegated sweep in [[docs/tasks/prose-style-sweep]]. Results are written to `wiki/lint/<YYYY-MM-DD>-lint.md`; log entries use `op: lint`. Routing lint failures block builds, not just produce reports, because routing drift propagates silently.
